  "body": "## Request\n\nAdd a post for Mach3 Mill (3-axis). Mach3 is still on many retrofit mills and routers. The `Mach4` post writes words and macros that Mach3 does not read in the same way.\n\n## Differences to the Mach4 post\n\nFrom the public \"Using Mach3Mill\" manual (behaviour only):\n\n- Canned cycles `G73`, `G81`, `G82`, `G83`, `G85`, `G86`, `G89` with `G98` / `G99`. `G84` needs a tapping head setup: refuse a tap by name unless the machine file says that tapping is available.\n- `G4 P` dwell: Mach3 reads P in seconds or in milliseconds by a setting (\"G04 dwell param in ms\"). The post must have an option for this. Default: seconds, as in the manual.\n- Tool change: `T<n> M6`, then `G43 H<n>`. The `M6` behaviour depends on the user's `M6Start` / `M6End` macros, so the post does not assume a retract inside `M6`. It retracts first.\n- Arcs: the IJ mode (absolute or incremental) is a Mach3 setting. The post must not depend on it: it writes the IJ mode word that the user selects in an option (default incremental IJ). The goldens must be checked against the manual for the word that Mach3 reads. A header comment names the mode.\n- No Custom Macro B (`#` variables in expressions). The Mach4 post uses `[...]` expressions in some places; the Mach3 post must write plain numbers only.\n- Program end `M30`. Comments `( )`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A built-in post `Mach3` and a machine file.\n- Goldens for contour, pocket, drill, peck, bore, two tools, and the dwell option in both modes.\n- A test that no `[` expression and no `#` variable is posted.\n\nI can do this work in a PR from my fork.\n",
